    {
      "beat_id": "B04B",
      "act": "MECHANISM",
      "narration_text": "There's one more distinction worth making. A seed does not remove randomness from the model. It gives the random number generator a fixed place to begin. From that starting point, the same sequence of pseudo-random choices is produced again. Change the starting point, and the sequence changes, even though the code, the probabilities, and the number of draws all stay the same. That is why seed seven and seed ninety-nine can produce different counts without representing different experiments. They are two repeatable runs of the same experiment, with different starting points. Reproducibility tells us what happened under those conditions. It does not tell us that those conditions produced the right answer.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "stagger",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": "start",
            "event": "The shared setup and two seed columns appear."
          },
          {
            "at": "middle",
            "event": "Illustrative draw rows reveal in both columns."
          },
          {
            "at": "end",
            "event": "The measured count outcomes and boundary statement remain visible."
          }
        ],
        "visual_intent": "Explanatory comparison of two seeded runs; sequence rows are illustrative, while the final counts are the verified outputs.",
